// Tree trunk and root material (M16). `kind: Plant`, not `Solid` — plant
// cells never move via the CA sweep either way, and `Plant` lets other
// rules key on "is this alive" without checking the material name. `Cell::
// aux` was originally reserved separately for `Plant` (a future per-cell
// growth stage) versus `Solid` (M17's anchor distance); that growth-stage
// use never materialized -- real per-tip growth state lives in `TreeState`/
// `Tip`/`RootTip` (see `plant.rs`) instead, which is where it needed to be
// anyway (attractor lists and channel strength don't fit in a `u16`). With
// the slot actually free, architecture item 9 extends M17's anchor-distance
// tracking to cover `Plant` too — an unsupported trunk now breaks and falls
// the same way an unsupported stone span does.
//
// Flammable with real numbers, same as oil: wood is what turns a grown
// forest into an actual Drossel-Schwabl fire per the plan. Burns cooler and
// longer than oil (a log burns for a while, not in a flash) and, being
// mostly solid material rather than a thin liquid film, catches less
// readily per neighbour-check — flammability tuned below oil's 0.5.
(
    name: "wood",
    display: "Wood",
    kind: Plant,
    // Walk-through and climbable, while it is alive. A growing tree is
    // scenery you move past and go up, not a wall you stop against -- the
    // gnome used to wedge against trunks with no way round and no way
    // through, since `mine` skips organism cells. Read only alongside
    // `Cell::organism_id() != 0`, so a `wood` wall someone painted is as
    // solid as it ever was, and so is anything a body drops here.
    climbable: true,
    density: 0.9,
    // **Eight bark bands of four tonal steps** — see `organism::PALETTE_BAND`
    // and `leaf.ron`'s longer note. Band 0 is the original palette
    // unchanged; a species with no declared `bark_bands` still draws
    // uniformly over the whole thing, as before.
    //
    // Bark carries less of the silhouette than foliage does, so this axis
    // was deliberately kept shorter than leaf's — four bands against six.
    // **Four is one band per species, and that is exactly too few**: with
    // `count: 2` ranges, four species cannot be disjoint in four bands, and
    // the shipped set overlapped on every pair (`tree` 0-1, `conifer` 1-2,
    // `shrub` and `creeper` both 2-3). Halving the ranges to `count: 1`
    // would have made them disjoint and is the wrong trade — `count` is
    // what `bark_band_for_density` spends to show a dense individual apart
    // from a pioneer *within* a species, and losing that costs more than
    // four extra colours do. So the axis is eight now and the intent above
    // is carried by hue instead of by length: the four new bands are quiet,
    // not loud.
    //
    // 4-5 are conifer's, moved off band 1 so it stops sharing with `tree`;
    // 6-7 are creeper's, which had no bark of its own at all.
    colors: [
        // 0: mid brown — the original palette
        (92, 64, 40),
        (105, 74, 46),
        (80, 55, 34),
        (114, 81, 51),
        // 1: dark red-brown — the dense end of `tree` (conifer moved to 4-5)
        (78, 48, 34),
        (92, 58, 42),
        (64, 38, 26),
        (104, 68, 50),
        // 2: near-black brown — old or wet bark
        (58, 42, 30),
        (70, 52, 38),
        (46, 33, 23),
        (82, 62, 46),
        // 3: grey-brown — birch, and most shrub stems
        (112, 100, 84),
        (128, 116, 98),
        (96, 85, 70),
        (142, 130, 112),
        // 4: rust — a pine's warm, plated bark
        (124, 76, 48),
        (142, 90, 60),
        (106, 62, 38),
        (158, 106, 74),
        // 5: plum-brown — the dense end of the same, spruce rather than pine
        (92, 60, 52),
        (106, 72, 62),
        (76, 48, 42),
        (120, 86, 76),
        // 6: olive-drab — a green stem that has only just gone woody
        (96, 92, 62),
        (110, 106, 74),
        (80, 76, 50),
        (124, 120, 88),
        // 7: pale green-grey — an old vine stem
        (118, 118, 102),
        (134, 134, 118),
        (100, 100, 86),
        (150, 150, 134),
    ],
    flammability: 0.25,
    // Autoignition, added with lava (the heat verb): until lava shipped
    // nothing in the world could raise a cell's *own* temperature without
    // already being on fire, so this was left at its "never" default and
    // `oil.ron`'s header says as much. Lava is exactly the case
    // `ignition_temperature`'s own doc names -- "contact with lava,
    // standing in a fire's heat" -- and without it a log dropped in a lava
    // flow sits there getting hot and never lights.
    //
    // 400, not the plan sketch's 300, and the difference is entirely about
    // what it does to *fire spread*, which is tuned against percolation and
    // was not up for retuning here. `fire::diffuse_heat` pulls a cell
    // toward the average of its four neighbours, so a wood cell's steady
    // state next to N cells pinned at wood's own 700C burn temperature,
    // with the rest at ambient 20, is (700N + 20(4-N)) / 4: one burning
    // neighbour 190C, two 360C, three 530C. At 300 a wood cell with two
    // burning neighbours would light deterministically and instantly,
    // which is a different fire model from the probabilistic contact
    // spread the flammability number above was tuned for; at 400 it takes
    // three, a configuration a spreading front rarely holds. Against lava
    // (pinned 1000C) the same arithmetic gives 265C for a lone cell
    // touching a flow -- which does *not* light, deliberately, a stray
    // splash should not torch a forest -- and 510C for a plank lying on
    // one (lava below, air above, warm wood either side), which does.
    // Measured, not just derived. `examples/ascii.rs` is byte-identical
    // across this change on every scene but one -- including "a worm
    // burrows through sand, then flees from fire" -- and on the one that
    // moved (the burned-tree regrowth scene) the *fire* did the same thing
    // either way: 666 ash before, 664 cells of wood consumed after. Only
    // the stochastic regrow half downstream of it landed differently, which
    // that scene's own comment already says it may.
    ignition_temperature: 400.0,
    burn_temperature: 700.0,
    burn_duration: 420,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.15,
    // The plan's own suggested number for wood specifically ("stone 3,
    // wood 8, steel 20") -- unused until item 9 gave `Plant` a place to
    // read it from.
    max_unsupported_span: 8,
    // Reach, not capacity -- see MaterialDef::max_cantilever_reach. Measured
    // max on a healthy 8-tree stand is 77; 96 is that with headroom.
    max_cantilever_reach: 96,
    breaks_into: "deadwood",
    // **The three tiers a felled tree comes apart into**, and the two lines
    // below are two of them. `Reports/physical-trees-design-2026-08-23.md`
    // §5: a real fell severed 2,648 cells and promoted 45 of them (1.7%) as
    // pieces, because the ladder wood drew from was rock's -- {2, 4, 8, 16,
    // 32} cells, two rungs of which are below `rigid::MIN_BODY_CELLS` and
    // can never be a piece at all. Everything else became single deadwood
    // grains and piled at their angle of repose. A cone of sawdust.
    //
    // 5 moves the base to {32, 64, 128, 256, 400} (the top rung capped by
    // `MAX_BODY_CELLS`), which is a bole, a limb, a branch, a stick. The
    // ceiling is deliberately *not* touched: a 2,400-cell crown wants to be
    // six to ten pieces and not one slab, which is the all-or-nothing
    // failure `design-philosophy.md` §0a names, approached from the other
    // side.
    fragment_floor: 5,
    // Grown at eight neighbours, so it fragments at eight, and severed it
    // comes away as pieces rather than as scatter. See `MaterialDef::woody`
    // -- both consequences are the same fact and the measurement for each
    // is there.
    woody: true,
    // The piece tier. `breaks_into` above is still the grit: which one a
    // cell gets is decided by whether the fragment it landed in cleared
    // `MIN_BODY_CELLS`, so the outcome is graded by the size of what broke
    // rather than by a switch.
    severs_into: "log",

    // **What it takes to snap a limb**, fitted to the *shape* of the stand
    // rather than to its worst cell -- which is the distinction that got
    // this constant wrong the first time.
    //
    // Censused on `scene=grove` with `BREAK=off`, so nothing could break
    // while it was being measured. Eight world seeds at 12,000 frames, the
    // peak wood stress of **each plant** pooled into one distribution --
    // **71 plants**:
    //
    //     p50 6,735   p75 10,094   p90 21,032   p95 22,925   max 31,160
    //
    // 21000 is that distribution's **p90**, so about a tenth of plants stand
    // over the line at any moment.
    //
    // **p75 was tried first and the stand did not survive it.** The owner
    // asked for p75 on a selection argument -- *"I expect plants to evolve
    // to be stronger and the damage rate will drop over time"* -- which is
    // sound and needs the population to live long enough to select. It does
    // not. One seed, 160,000 frames, `scene=grove`, living plant tissue,
    // against a `BREAK=off` control on the same seed:
    //
    //     frames    control    p90 (this)      p75
    //      20,000    17,336        17,476   16,898
    //      60,000    19,194        17,085    8,790
    //     100,000    18,583        15,858    7,763
    //     160,000    19,123        14,859    4,057
    //     snaps           -            16      278
    //     plants          9             7        5
    //
    // At p75 the wood loses **79%** of itself and the stand falls from nine
    // plants to five. That is not the lifecycle -- the control holds flat at
    // ~19,000 -- and evolution cannot answer it, because **a population
    // culled faster than it breeds crashes before selection can operate**.
    // The owner's mechanism requires the stand to persist, so this is an
    // argument for the gentler constant rather than against the idea.
    //
    // At p90 the stand holds at ~78% of the control and still loses sixteen
    // limbs over the run. The per-plant peak drifts down across it (p90
    // 15,306 -> 10,446), which is *consistent with* selection on
    // `pipe_ratio` and is not evidence of it: culling the worst-proportioned
    // trees produces the same trend, and separating the two needs the
    // genotype tracked across generations rather than the phenotype
    // censused.
    //
    // **Flat per material, with no wood-density term, and that is what aims
    // the pressure at growth patterns.** Scaling it by
    // `organism::wood_density` was built and removed on the owner's call:
    // *"I care more about mechanics impacting growth patterns so I don't
    // want density dominating this evolutionary pressure."* Density is a
    // **discrete** allele worth a straight 1.8x on the threshold
    // (`WOOD_DENSITY_ALLELES` 0.75..1.35), so one mutation buys the whole
    // escape and shape never has to change.
    //
    // Section is the steeper gradient anyway: doubling a trunk's girth
    // *quarters* its stress. Girth is `pipe_ratio` at genome slot 4 --
    // "nodes of foliage per cell of stem width" -- whose `genotype_variance`
    // is **0.7, the widest of any trait in `tree.ron`'s tuple**. Density
    // keeps a job regardless: it still scales `max_cantilever_reach` on the
    // span rule in `structural.rs`.
    //
    // **The first version of this number was 60,000 and it was fitted to the
    // wrong statistic.** It came from the *stand* maximum -- one cell out of
    // eight thousand, which belongs to the single worst-grown individual in
    // the wood, i.e. exactly the tree that ought to fail. A strength set
    // above that is set above the whole failure mode: nothing ever breaks,
    // wind can never break anything either (the ceiling it was measured
    // against already had a gust in it), and the outcome is the binary
    // `CLAUDE.md`'s ethos forbids. Owner's correction: *"a tree that grows
    // poorly then that it's a heavy top should be able to break on its
    // own"*.
    //
    // **What the criterion actually discriminates.** `stress` is
    // `|moment| / section^2`, so a heavy crown on a narrow base reads high
    // and a big *well-proportioned* tree does not -- girth is in the
    // denominator, squared. That is what makes "poorly grown" a measurable
    // thing rather than a synonym for "large".
    //
    // `rootwood` and `leaf` are deliberately left without one. Roots are
    // underground and take no wind, and a leaf that snapped under load would
    // be a leaf falling off its branch -- the exact complaint the foliage
    // work exists to answer.
    strength: 21000.0,
)
